  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Zn + 2HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂; the metal is above hydrogen in the series, so it displaces the gas.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Hydrogen is the lightest gas, so it rises into an inverted jar; it can also be collected over water because it is almost insoluble.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A burning splint at the mouth of the jar ignites the gas with a sharp pop.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    CH₄ + H₂O → CO + 3H₂ over a nickel catalyst at about 800 °C, followed by a shift reaction.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Hydrogen adds to the double bonds of unsaturated oils, converting them into semi-solid fats.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Liquid air is distilled, and oxygen is drawn off at its boiling point of −183 °C.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    2H₂O₂ → 2H₂O + O₂; the manganese(IV) oxide speeds the decomposition and is unchanged.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A glowing splint bursts into flame in oxygen because the gas supports combustion.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Its low solubility means little is lost, and the water column shows when the jar is full.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Nitrogen is the main component of air, with oxygen at about 21 %.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The N≡N bond has a very high bond energy, so nitrogen needs extreme conditions to react.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Nitrogen boils at −196 °C and oxygen at −183 °C, so the more volatile nitrogen distils first.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Its very low boiling point makes it useful for freezing food and for storing biological samples.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    2NH₄Cl + Ca(OH)₂ → CaCl₂ + 2NH₃ + 2H₂O; an ammonium salt plus a base releases ammonia.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ammonia reacts with both sulphuric acid and calcium chloride, so the basic drying agent calcium oxide is used instead.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ammonia is less dense than air, so the jar is held mouth-downwards and the gas rises into it.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ammonia is the only common alkaline gas; it also gives white smoke of ammonium chloride with hydrogen chloride.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A drop of water drawn into a flask of ammonia dissolves the gas and the pressure falls, drawing a fountain of water up.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Finely divided iron catalyses the combination of nitrogen and hydrogen.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A moderate temperature and a high pressure balance yield against rate.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    By Le Chatelier's principle, pressure drives the equilibrium towards the side with fewer gas molecules.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A compromise is made between the thermodynamic yield and the rate at which it is reached.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ammonia is oxidised over platinum–rhodium to nitrogen(II) oxide, which is then converted to nitric acid.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ammonia is burnt in air over a platinum–rhodium gauze at about 900 °C.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    2NO + O₂ → 2NO₂; the gas turns brown as the nitrogen dioxide forms.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Nitric acid is an oxidising acid: Cu + 4HNO₃ → Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2NO₂ + 2H₂O.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The nitrate ion is reduced in preference to the hydrogen ion, so the gas evolved is a nitrogen oxide.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Iron(II) sulphate and concentrated sulphuric acid give a brown ring of the nitroso compound at the junction of the layers.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Urea is the most concentrated solid nitrogen fertiliser, at about 46 % nitrogen.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The Haber process fixes atmospheric nitrogen industrially; nitrification converts ammonium to nitrate in the soil.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Denitrifying bacteria reduce nitrate to nitrogen, which returns to the atmosphere.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The nutrients feed an algal bloom, which later decays and removes the dissolved oxygen.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, so 1.0 mol of nitrogen gives 2.0 mol of ammonia, which occupies 44.8 dm³.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    n(H₂) = 3.0 mol, and the ratio is 3 : 2, so 2.0 mol of ammonia is formed, which has a mass of 34 g.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    n(H₂O₂) = 68 ÷ 34 = 2.0 mol, and 2H₂O₂ gives 1O₂, so 1.0 mol of oxygen occupies 22.4 dm³.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    M(NH₄NO₃) = 80, and the two nitrogen atoms contribute 28, so the percentage is 28 ÷ 80 × 100 = 35 %.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ammonia is basic and reacts with the acid, so an acidic drying agent cannot be used.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Carbon dioxide is denser than air, so it is delivered downwards into the jar; hydrogen, ammonia and methane are all lighter than air.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    C + H₂O → CO + H₂; the mixture burns with a blue flame and is used as a fuel.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O; the flame is pale blue and almost invisible in daylight.
  ]

]

